<section>
	<h2>A Brief History of the Crusades</h2>
	<p>To date, four crusades have been launched against the demons of the Worldwound, and while all four have had varying
		degrees of success and failure, none have yet driven the armies of Deskari, Lord of the Locust Host, from Golarion.
		A brief history of the war follows. </p>
	<div class="floatswitch">
		<section class="history">
			<h3>First Crusade (4622 AR - 4630 AR)</h3>
			<p>In 4433, an avatar of the <b>Ascendent Aroden, the Last Azlanti,</b> appeared in <b>Sarkoris</b> to warn the
				divided clansman of the threat posed by the demon lord <b>Deskari</b>. His cultists spread his faith throughout
				their nation as <i>god-callers</i>, and had spread corruption so thoroughly amongst the people that Deskari was
				able to use their faith to create the first rift at the <b>Lake of Mists and Veils</b>. Calling the clansman
				across Sarkoris and <b>Mendev</b> to war, his forces cut through the demonic swarm where Aroden slayed the
				avatar of Deskari. They then drove the demonic host before them back into the lake, where they squirmed back
				through the rift or drowned. He then sealed the rift with his holy power and vowed to personally keep Deskari
				sealed away.
			</p>
			<p>In 4606, the death of Aroden sent much of Golarion and the cosmos reeling, ushering in the <b>Age of Lost
					Omens</b>. Deskari, no longer hiding from the wrath of the Last Azlanti, and having spent the last centuries
				rebuilding his power, threw open a new rift in reality. This ruptured the boundary between Sarkoris and his
				abyssal realm, the <b>Rasping Rifts</b>, blighting the land and vomiting forth an endless stream of Deskari's
				demons who descended on the unsuspecting Sarkorian clansman. Refugees spilled across the border, particularly
				into nearby Mendev, but the demon host was one of just many problems to emerge following Aroden's death. The
				neighboring nations did not properly grasp the threat until in early 4607 AR, when the lilitu demon
				<b>Minagho</b> entered the Mendevian town of <b>Kenabres</b> disguised as a refugee, where she slaughtered 62
				civilians before <i>vanishing in a cloud of smoke</i> in a moment known as the <b>Red Morning Massacre</b>.
			</p>
			<p>The young <b>Queen Galifrey</b> was an untested paladin of Aroden who worried after her people, when she was
				called by the <b>Inheritor</b> to defend her kingdom of Mendev from the locust horde. She took up
				<b>Iomedae's</b> banner and immediately sent for aid, with a few holy knights answering the call, particularly
				from amongst the <b>Knights of Ozem</b> in <b>Lastwall</b>, but their numbers were not enough to stem the
				surging tide. The church of Iomedae did not declare the <b>First Crusade</b> until 4622 AR, as the repercussions
				of Aroden's death significantly delayed the church's ability to respond to the growing crisis. By the time the
				First Crusade reached Mendev, uniting Arodenites and newly converted Iomedaeans by the same holy cause, the
				demons had long since seized control of central Sarkoris, and had claimed significant portions of Mendev.
			</p>
			<p>The First Crusade swelled the defenders ranks, and they were soon joined by powerful allies, such as the
				ancient silver dragon <b>Terendelev</b>, the great phoenix <b>Pyralisia</b>, and Iomedae's new herald, the
				<b>Hand of the Inheritor</b>. The sudden increase in the enemy's strength caught the demonic horde unprepared,
				causing them to retreat back to the <b>Northmounds</b>. With Mendev and southern Sarkoris thus liberated, the
				crusaders remained in the region to help rebuild - an offer Mendev welcomed gratefully, but one that the
				disparate and proud clans of Sarkoris accepted more reluctantly. In any event, for the next few years the demons
				seemed content to focus their wrath upon Sarkoris's Northmounds, battling primarily with the surviving Sarkorian
				clans desperately attempting to reclaim their family lands, while Mendev remained relatively unmolested.
			</p>
			<p>Kenabres and <b>Nerosyan</b> were fortified into great cities, and the indomitable crusader fortress of
				<b>Drezen</b> was constructed within these contested lands on Mendevian soil. It became a symbol of the
				crusade's might, purpose, audacity, and ambition. But these victories came at the cost of countless lives, and
				its scars cut across generations as the children of crusaders were born infected with an abyssal taint. Twisted
				and deformed, these offspring were persecuted by the frightened, demon scarred populace of Mendev and Sarkoris.
				To keep them safe, their parents fled underground with their children, disappearing into the safety of half
				remembered legend.
			</p>
		</section>
		<section class="history">
			<h3>Second Crusade (4638 AR - 4645 AR)</h3>
			<p>Deskari was not defeated, but simply biding his time, allowing his enemies to think his forces as a mindless,
				contained threat. When a second wave of demons erupted from the Worldwound in 4636 AR, the crusaders had settled
				into their new homes in Mendev. They again took up arms against the demons, expecting a short series of fights
				and boasting that this time they would drive the host back to the very edges of the Worldwound itself. But
				their expectations did not come to fruition. This time, the demons pouring from the Worldwound were not only
				more numerous - they were better prepared. Rather than the haphazard, chaotic, self-indulgent mob the crusaders
				previously encountered, the marauding demons were now legions driven by powerful commanders. Under their
				commanders' direction, the demons orchestrated strike forces, teleported behind enemy lines, drove their enemies
				toward their advancing ranks, and then crushed their opponents between them. The armies of the marilith demon
				<b>Aponavicius</b> captured the crusader city of Drezen using such tactics, along with Iomedae's personal war
				banner, the <i>Sword of Valor</i>, forcing the church of Iomedae to finally call for the <b>Second Crusade</b>.
			</p>
			<p>Even with the influx of troops from the Second Crusade, however, it quickly became apparent the demons were
				going to win. Fortunately for Mendev, the demons aimed the bulk of their devastating attack westward and
				southward. The impending loss prompted the leaders of the Mendevian Crusaders to make a fateful decision to
				pull their support from Sarkoris, allowing the demon army to descend on what remained of that land. Sarkorian
				refugees spilled across the Sellen river and delivered a powerful but inactive relic, the <i>demonscope</i> to
				<b>Saint Clydwell</b>, champion of Aroden, who martyred his life in a fight against demons to activate it and
				create a hope for the crusade. The <i>demonscope</i> captured the soul of Saint Clydwell, along with the
				<i>quintessence</i> of the balor lord <b>Goriath</b> and his attendant demons, trapping them inside in an
				endless, deathless battle.
			</p>
			<p>His three most loyal knights oversaw the erection of a field of menhir <i>wardstones</i> along the West Sellen
				and <b>Moutray</b> rivers. Then, under the guidance of the Hand of the Inheritor, they performed a ritual to
				channel her divine power into these <i>wardstones</i>, with the <i>demonscope</i> in the Kite of Kenabres as the
				focus, to create a holy barrier against the abyssal horde. The price of their actions proved steep. Many
				crusaders and divine warriors, including the great phoenix Pyralisia, died to buy the time needed, with her
				rebirth permanently corrupted by abyssal magics. But, as the <i>wardstones</i> flared to life, these menhirs
				contained the demons within lost Sarkoris and saved tens of thousands from grisly deaths. Yet despite this
				success, the near-total loss of Sarkoris is generally regarded as the final capstone on a disastrous crusade.
			</p>
		</section>
		<section class="history">
			<h3>Third Crusade (4665 AR - 4668 AR)</h3>
			<p>Now contained within Sarkoris by a combination of the <i>wardstones</i>, increased pressure from the <b>Mammoth
					Lords</b>, and the distraction of an entire nation to plunder, the demons continued to press against the
				borders but seemed largely content to revel in their captured realm. Meanwhile, as the years passed, the
				Mendevian crusaders grew more and more corrupt - in part due to the subtle machinations of the <b>Cult of
					Baphomet</b>, which had successfully infiltrated numerous companies and faiths throughout Mendev, but also
				because the resource-strained church of Iomedae had increasingly accepted less trustworthy members into its war
				effort. The church called the <b>Third Crusade</b> primarily as an attempt to galvanize the crusaders, but the
				long shadow of the Red Morning Massacre and the swift discovery of cultists in positions of power increasingly
				turned the focus of the crusade towards self-destructive <i>witch hunts</i> and internal squabbling, where
				overzealous and paranoid inquisitors burned the innocent alongside the guilty. It took three long years for the
				fires of the Third Crusade to burn out before it collapsed under its own corrupt weight. Ultimately, the Third
				Crusade accomplished very little within the Worldwound.</p>
		</section>
		<section class="history">
			<h3>Fourth Crusade (4692 AR - 4707 AR)</h3>
			<p>Deskari was not idle during this time. After decades of Abyssal rule, he recruits the mighty balor lord,
				<b>Khorramzadeh the Storm King</b> and ruler of the Sarkorian capital of <b>Iz</b>. His first assault on the
				border caught the crusaders off guard, and resulted in no less catastrophic an event than the <i>Cracking of the
					Kenabres wardstone</i> before Terendelev was able to drive him off. In response, the church of Iomedae called
				for the <b>Fourth Crusade</b>, but mercenaries and <b>"low" templars</b> outweigh the true believers, with many
				feeling the character of the crusades had changed. This crusade proved to be the longest and most grueling of
				the crusades yet, lasting 15 years and petering out more as a result of wartime exhaustion than anything else.
				The demons lost very little during this uneasy stalemate, and morale along the Worldwound's borders has reached
				an all-time low. Some maintain with conviction that the crusade is still in full force, with crusader camps and
				mercenaries trickling into Kenabres and Nerosyan ceaselessly, but they do not march into the Worldwound in
				force, instead merely gathering and going on occasional patrol. There is an uneasy quiet now, and a weary and
				demoralized Crusade is barely held together by the ageless, tireless, indefatigable leadership of Queen
				Galifrey, who inherited the throne at the age of fifteen, and whose reign has seen over one hundred years of
				war.
			</p>
		</section>
	</div>
</section>